# TeX and TikZ

The `tex` verbatim environment hands its contents to a real LaTeX engine.
SvelTeX compiles the document, converts it to SVG with `dvisvgm`, and inlines
the result — so these are genuine TikZ pictures, not raster images.

## A labelled figure

<tex ref="showcase-figure">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \draw (0,0) circle (1.6);
    \draw[dashed, var(--some-color), thick] (-2,-2) rectangle (2,2);
    \draw[thick] (-1.6,0) -- (1.6,0);
    \node at (0,0.7) {$\displaystyle \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathrm{d}\mathbf{r}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</tex>

The dashed border above is drawn in the page's `--some-color` CSS variable:
SvelTeX keeps `var(--…)` references intact in the generated SVG, so the figure
picks up its colour from the surrounding stylesheet.

## A small graph

A second `tex` block — each figure is compiled independently:

<tex ref="showcase-graph">
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={circle, draw, minimum size=8mm}]
    \node (a) at (90:1.4) {$a$};
    \node (b) at (210:1.4) {$b$};
    \node (c) at (330:1.4) {$c$};
    \draw (a) -- (b) -- (c) -- (a);
\end{tikzpicture}
</tex>

The `tex` environment, with its TikZ preamble, is declared in the `verbatim`
section of `sveltex.config.js`.
